<article class="env-corollary" id="A675">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/A675">Corollary <span data-tag="A675">6.3.5.9</span>.</a> Suppose given a commutative diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { &  \calY \ar [dr]^{g_{\ast }} &  \\ \calX \ar [ur]^{f_{\ast }} \ar [rr]^{h_{\ast }} &  &  \calZ } \]
</div>
  <p> in $\LGeom ^{op}$, where $g_{\ast }$ is étale. Then $f_{\ast }$ is étale if and only if $h_{\ast }$ is étale. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The “only if” direction is obvious. To prove the converse, let us suppose that $g_{\ast }$ and $h_{\ast }$ are both étale, so that we have equivalences $\calX \simeq \calZ _{/U}$ and $\calU \simeq \calZ _{/V}$ for some pair of objects $U, V \in \calZ $. Using Remark <a href="/tag/1A21" data-tag="1A21">6.3.5.7</a>, we deduce that the morphism $f_{\ast }$ is determined by a map $U \rightarrow V$ in $\calZ $, which we can identify with an object $\overline{V} \in \calY $ such that $\calX \simeq \calY _{/ \overline{V} }$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>